WEBVTT

00:01.007 --> 00:03.492
- [Instructor] Divya received
the following problem.

00:03.492 --> 00:05.419
A particle moves in a
straight line with velocity

00:05.419 --> 00:07.370
v of t is equal to the square root

00:07.370 --> 00:09.622
of three t minus one meters per second,

00:09.622 --> 00:11.781
where t is time in seconds.

00:11.781 --> 00:13.082
At t equals two,

00:13.082 --> 00:15.172
the particle's distance
from the starting point

00:15.172 --> 00:18.887
was eight meters in
the positive direction.

00:18.887 --> 00:22.741
What is the particle's position
at t equals seven seconds?

00:22.741 --> 00:25.690
Which expression should Divya
use to solve the problem?

00:25.690 --> 00:27.896
So pause this video and have a go at it.

00:27.896 --> 00:30.659
Alright now let's do this together.

00:30.659 --> 00:34.560
So we wanna know the particle's position

00:34.560 --> 00:36.813
at t is equal to seven.

00:36.813 --> 00:38.020
So what we could do,

00:38.020 --> 00:41.898
they tell us what our
position is at t equals two,

00:41.898 --> 00:44.173
so what the position at

00:44.173 --> 00:46.101
t equals seven would be your position

00:46.101 --> 00:47.768
at t equals two plus

00:49.584 --> 00:50.501
your change

00:52.091 --> 00:53.008
in position

00:55.551 --> 00:56.968
from t equals two

00:59.591 --> 01:01.508
to t is equal to seven.

01:02.656 --> 01:04.909
And there's another word for this.

01:04.909 --> 01:07.045
You could also call this your displacement

01:07.045 --> 01:10.045
from t equals two to t equals seven.

01:10.992 --> 01:13.709
And we know how to think
about displacement.

01:13.709 --> 01:17.889
Velocity is your rate of
change of displacement.

01:17.889 --> 01:20.071
And so if you wanna figure
out your displacement,

01:20.071 --> 01:22.463
your between two times,

01:22.463 --> 01:24.785
you would integrate the velocity function.

01:24.785 --> 01:26.527
So this is going to be the integral

01:26.527 --> 01:29.290
from t equals two to t equals seven

01:29.290 --> 01:33.167
of our velocity function, v of t, dt.

01:33.167 --> 01:36.232
This would be our displacement
from time two to time seven.

01:36.232 --> 01:37.765
So if they said what is
our change in position

01:37.765 --> 01:39.228
from time two to time seven,

01:39.228 --> 01:41.271
it would be just this expression.

01:41.271 --> 01:42.363
But they want us,

01:42.363 --> 01:43.361
they want us to figure out,

01:43.361 --> 01:44.731
or they want Divya to figure out,

01:44.731 --> 01:47.564
what is the particle's position
at t equals seven seconds.

01:47.564 --> 01:49.096
So what you'd wanna do is your position

01:49.096 --> 01:50.722
at t equals two,

01:50.722 --> 01:52.997
and we know what our
position at t equals two is.

01:52.997 --> 01:55.203
It was eight meters in
the positive direction,

01:55.203 --> 01:57.548
so we could just call that
positive eight meters.

01:57.548 --> 01:59.778
So it's going to be eight plus

01:59.778 --> 02:01.937
your change in position,

02:01.937 --> 02:03.655
which is going to be your displacement.

02:03.655 --> 02:05.954
And let's see we can see this choice

02:05.954 --> 02:08.137
right over there,

02:08.137 --> 02:09.646
and that's what we would pick.

02:09.646 --> 02:10.853
This first option,

02:10.853 --> 02:13.222
v of seven, that just gives us our

02:13.222 --> 02:15.660
velocity at time seven,

02:15.660 --> 02:18.818
or at the exactly at seven seconds.

02:18.818 --> 02:22.951
Or another way, our rate
of change of displacement

02:22.951 --> 02:24.925
at seven seconds.

02:24.925 --> 02:26.341
So that's not what we want.

02:26.341 --> 02:27.572
This one right over here,

02:27.572 --> 02:29.731
you have your position

02:29.731 --> 02:31.612
at t equals two,

02:31.612 --> 02:33.191
but then you have your change in position

02:33.191 --> 02:36.651
from t equals zero to t equals seven.

02:36.651 --> 02:39.042
So this doesn't seem, this isn't right.

02:39.042 --> 02:42.224
And this is your position at time two

02:42.224 --> 02:43.724
plus your v prime,

02:45.126 --> 02:47.727
the derivative of velocity
is the acceleration,

02:47.727 --> 02:49.422
plus your acceleration at time seven,

02:49.422 --> 02:51.094
so that's definitely not gonna give you

02:51.094 --> 02:53.880
your the particle's position.

02:53.880 --> 02:54.958
So we like that second choice.
